[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9f1302-a225-50dc-8282-9cdc30e460cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276298} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5a4698-2fec-5e90-91df-84219743e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276727} {:answer/id #uuid "92236399-f0b6-5795-b074-da5f2cda69c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276800} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc48f93-0f2e-5c19-8450-cae78ea3b874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8af4d1-9fe9-507f-bcdf-572cdb0ebc23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2253cfff-ee0a-5c15-93af-d26f561ec17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158603" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892a8ae7-7816-5ec8-abd7-0f193b914c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276716} {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276853} {:answer/id #uuid "cef0cd2a-9051-554c-a6f5-91d00529319a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277308} {:answer/id #uuid "e65fda98-7388-56e8-99ca-9bfe1594e4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277488} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc62238-282a-5ff2-b84f-201b8a5eed6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276853} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd602e17-33cb-5cf6-97db-92756d77aaff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed33ac2f-6299-5342-b520-1399b2a77786" :question/math-academy-id "q-158684" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0243a0f1-2e03-5947-a203-a74798f52121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275498} {:answer/id #uuid "1d167fdd-7c58-55ea-9621-e7c510872897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275743} {:answer/id #uuid "227d62f9-8ba3-5bb9-80bd-c1da5585045a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275786} {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277634} {:answer/id #uuid "fed55126-d8c5-5c16-a6ce-4064c6e9eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277634} :answer-field/id #uuid "487c91f9-e32d-5423-97c7-0f4657cf1eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1861489-bfb5-5c9b-ba84-97990e733cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158685" :question/problem "![](images/73/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](images/84/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](images/e5/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b91dfb0-ae09-545f-ac7a-c0d00abc2f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275733} {:answer/id #uuid "5e409313-241a-511e-b50f-1b0d65407b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276319} {:answer/id #uuid "6f534c7a-cb11-5b8b-8823-ce6135e59b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276478} {:answer/id #uuid "747d36d6-b304-566e-bae8-47d115a5aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276531} {:answer/id #uuid "c346c257-8a91-52ac-af3f-17f228f8abce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "747d36d6-b304-566e-bae8-47d115a5aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276531} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7281777-c50f-510c-980a-e03bcfd2b780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98673ae2-3448-5c6b-87b7-226f4a36e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158731" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}\\ge -2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}=-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} {:answer/id #uuid "4140e559-6092-5ae2-8bcd-a98f2357d749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276050} {:answer/id #uuid "42bb2cbf-5462-514b-b822-00b92a2500dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276065} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4265f7-2803-5a4c-af0b-23d6cfcd9d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277152} {:answer/id #uuid "e710bfdf-fe8b-54d4-91db-e06b6c274c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ce3a08-8352-57fb-9450-c1e8717cb404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d4676b-36dc-5235-80c5-02c2a6d333fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158733" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3585e5cf-97a2-5a9f-9483-1363128dc2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275955} {:answer/id #uuid "72c53d80-bb03-50ef-96b9-e5708290dc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276517} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffb6950-d856-55a4-a552-464de9c420fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276779} {:answer/id #uuid "bd749509-056c-5495-87c6-08d0205808fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277167} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277325} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49af98b-856c-574a-b392-0ae041c650d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94c1ba54-750c-5dd8-b08c-c465652d3c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159264" :question/problem "![](images/07/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside $M.$\n\n![](images/73/734ff08a25e25486dc65f919ce99bc5422ca8ce264bb7d044479b1eb3b6a716b.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/28/286f8b397b3c908d29678640d691558a784e31751d09c68e6b6da855c272231c.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/7e/7ed7726fa1a139ded4a9226bcb01c17d0ad46ef4bd03a820f033640c68778453.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcda877-ce32-54bb-86fa-841eb47431e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275590} {:answer/id #uuid "acc485f4-60b3-5514-a8d1-316701fb6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277031} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a14746-068d-52e8-8dbf-99390fdab2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277088} {:answer/id #uuid "d25a5ddc-5600-5cdb-8e5a-06d78eaba4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277333} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c7f128-018d-5267-98fd-8c3e16aa45a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc485f4-60b3-5514-a8d1-316701fb6d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72f9d85-496b-5a8f-a884-7d71c4e09859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae427c1b-c208-5c4a-a164-5e046d8d2ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159274" :question/problem "![](images/43/43380e1382df5db66c4a9ab0ee385e98073da7fb684ccdf93a665e223fb1a554.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty ,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c6/c66edbc427dbd1e970a5c25422fbfca39296b0a90b6ca26dacb3280551105784.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/7b/7b8beea4c575e89b2c66f363271a90068e912422ad8991a333375f6894523a57.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a06851f25bf80edcc934fe7f341a6a43a065e744691164d46f77d116d4bf047.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100300ab-7a48-59a3-8f5c-174ca08e7765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275616} {:answer/id #uuid "15c34ad8-fa0b-5a75-8eb4-85368db54d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275679} {:answer/id #uuid "34416daf-38dd-57f1-8f28-56a3e47bcfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275942} {:answer/id #uuid "501c4afd-a964-52cf-bd2c-92914657e867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276195} {:answer/id #uuid "87df0387-b2a0-58e7-ac29-1982d729669c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c34ad8-fa0b-5a75-8eb4-85368db54d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275679} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d8f0289-9a31-5539-9fae-2ed2de52f41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "951baf6c-974c-552a-a084-e87ba6cba5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159485" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ad8b759d3f2781b1dc6adf64e58a230218655249f21fdd8f6a852568554ead.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/56/56a640baf27e1acb5c3428e1bef25a87b3ff965dff71762b94fb1eff94aaf24d.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048011b9-ffee-53bf-ac13-2e97743e225c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275519} {:answer/id #uuid "0efa31d6-31db-55c2-9b88-0a9a3a3453fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275607} {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276052} {:answer/id #uuid "4305747f-a88f-529f-a758-988093ddc9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276069} {:answer/id #uuid "eda63547-3ece-56ee-9327-e038a1f457a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417cc1c5-3d24-5fc5-a84c-b4dc4ea116ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276052} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bae57d-74be-5ed9-b597-76e04905579c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e85ebcb1-0815-545b-a7ea-7afc898a561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159502" :question/problem "![](images/79/79fc33355e32fc3b57a129e83fdf35ecdd1e5b0f3275c433a49d85642cf40f3f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a7/a7f99060032c4f79df8b2ee68e220f31bb14f29f99e9190c9b385d073f3274d9.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a87d209-f254-5e99-94a3-0ddb685b4606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275867} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab69bab-cd54-568d-a211-6e10a07499f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275992} {:answer/id #uuid "6eef0fc7-bda9-5526-bdb2-5aeceb0f1904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276472} {:answer/id #uuid "90955a99-dd82-5134-a08a-bdd5c1bddd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276787} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2f0be8-be9d-5c84-a2ec-b3ae58f612fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90955a99-dd82-5134-a08a-bdd5c1bddd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276787} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b53aa9-c9a6-5c2c-9e5b-5a42d90582fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9430b4bf-f1ad-5467-9486-5b90e2e64bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159550" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e999290971eed02d7f12f9154bdb4d04f3328fd250b385059d94e83e3f195b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f10ff87f64dc07adb8e9535926186341bab88f2d0c783475ef8fd57b06b35fc0.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0901f9cc-2e85-5737-94a9-7b639e5a3628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275555} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec853ad-ed6a-516c-8ae4-5a8f901436a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276177} {:answer/id #uuid "c86b1d1d-b0a5-5a0f-a4a7-754c482594b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277259} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cf432e-e934-591a-abcc-649994f1b071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277351} {:answer/id #uuid "de2694b0-4992-5426-80dd-680a5a3f1829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4cf432e-e934-591a-abcc-649994f1b071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e236d5-38a4-59ff-bb41-dcbe9764411d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dee7594-767a-521f-928c-9f8f15efe4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275750} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb2e03d-9953-5eb9-a536-ad69732a1f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275903} {:answer/id #uuid "5e17f6c4-abcb-5795-b673-23aac5d88fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276316} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad87c08-849b-5dd3-b088-825965a6f51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276436} {:answer/id #uuid "ef719bcd-1195-59b2-a5bc-eeccf0b0f347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad87c08-849b-5dd3-b088-825965a6f51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276436} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e3bd56-16a0-5dc4-9fa7-2fc1cdf50250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13406d62-3495-5617-8c59-07fc2d20bdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159977" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,4),(0,2),$ and $(7,10).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(2,3)$\n- $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,1),(-1,2),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130ffd37-1468-59cd-96b1-b10e7bdf65ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275651} {:answer/id #uuid "6859ae0e-d44d-5274-9b9e-d7e68347e033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276410} {:answer/id #uuid "9abd85c9-71b3-56c3-8aa1-d23dcc5bc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276863} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea5b85-addc-5034-aa5a-a73a7c6981d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276992} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7813b3-c467-5163-8ec3-ddced4315606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9abd85c9-71b3-56c3-8aa1-d23dcc5bc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276863} :answer-field/id #uuid "55beb8be-bd4d-5ffb-803e-4528647a6e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0b402cc-9d61-5ea9-b280-42fd73163096" :question/math-academy-id "q-159978" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(7,5),$ and $(2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(0,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9-8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(1,1),(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e96e870-a8af-5662-990f-06837d1c1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276021} {:answer/id #uuid "a084d05c-5854-5b46-818f-33c21866514c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276923} {:answer/id #uuid "b88e3577-8da0-530e-96a8-50f04a54528a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277112} {:answer/id #uuid "d86d5412-4be8-5eb1-9303-99785d92c929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277380} {:answer/id #uuid "db95dfe7-00c7-5ec1-9e3f-e75c11dc2fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b88e3577-8da0-530e-96a8-50f04a54528a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277112} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9639c91-0add-57a7-80f2-ad69ca23cf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf3cb2bb-bb5c-564c-af6e-ef65a9ae1e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-160368" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0),(-1,0),(2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(-1,0),(-2,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289d7999-7a6b-5687-a2ef-02f8b473d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275845} {:answer/id #uuid "81c07be2-1172-50f7-a04c-94df6d7a277e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276647} {:answer/id #uuid "b18abb20-e495-5103-863f-119ae8db6fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277066} {:answer/id #uuid "d44f9e45-37ec-5a84-b576-6c21b41c0a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277347} {:answer/id #uuid "e74a3358-871b-50ec-b11f-4fa504902a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18abb20-e495-5103-863f-119ae8db6fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277066} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b5dc04-4d52-5d46-b88e-b20b9902f553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fb94a38-de9a-5a83-b5a3-84ca0f8fdab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160369" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,5),(4,-3),(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,5)$\n- $(-4,-3)$\n- $(-3,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-5),(-4,3),(-3,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de2521b-1eec-563c-85f4-ea0a7a5e2ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276315} {:answer/id #uuid "68e23fa0-c706-5d3c-9dea-e5bffba2edd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276419} {:answer/id #uuid "6f26ba0c-8043-55c7-bcd3-aba9f943f97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276474} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6081c3-c38a-5b5d-925d-c7f55c991451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277698} {:answer/id #uuid "ff041318-9ffe-52c8-84b2-c84d717421f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de2521b-1eec-563c-85f4-ea0a7a5e2ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276315} :answer-field/id #uuid "656c65fc-9e17-5d9c-8874-1ca316880de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "213f7139-636c-56af-8c2a-7163cc744332" :question/math-academy-id "q-160371" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b97fdd-ec15-5024-a675-6f18d78a504f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275937} {:answer/id #uuid "72a79a05-3ff8-5e96-bcb2-e8a8ff867510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276515} {:answer/id #uuid "7711ff19-0075-5799-8216-046f734fa934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276553} {:answer/id #uuid "8e00bb97-add7-5acf-8a4e-5a5680f62f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276766} {:answer/id #uuid "a6968b4d-0033-5df2-9d13-63334bda3e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a79a05-3ff8-5e96-bcb2-e8a8ff867510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276515} :answer-field/id #uuid "379ed9a7-417d-594f-ba8d-c45f27b16100" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff0f96a5-d30c-508e-bdde-b67be8bf18d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160374" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b4da5d-1547-5e75-a0c1-db44d993e711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275541} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3aa1f-7b6e-539c-8ca0-13d548dcfd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275905} {:answer/id #uuid "774a27e9-495e-5ec6-a4c4-eb40083b9df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276557} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2bcb50-dc39-55fc-813b-bc57e9fba2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276860} {:answer/id #uuid "a196150c-05e4-526a-aa0e-20293c61db43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774a27e9-495e-5ec6-a4c4-eb40083b9df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276557} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b74de4-a3f0-5fa5-9815-5929160c53c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ef2dcb7-ec09-542d-a947-352534dabf78" :question/math-academy-id "q-160399" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $3+4\\text{i}$\n- $-2+3\\text{i}$\n- $1-2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $3+4\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=3$ and the imaginary part $y=4.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(3,4)$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+3\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=3.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,3)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $1-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=1$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(1,-2)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](images/a4/a4f87e680d46b099b9543b552191c776ac7145fba4e1b51b10c44a3685907d46.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74fece1e-fbf6-5fd9-9e1a-5416ec62c323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276536} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b6c80a-c282-540a-bd6d-4542edbded5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276953} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb85f95-c186-5d26-b937-d9f1355f0b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277432} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa639f-56f1-511f-917a-859bb168c38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277444} {:answer/id #uuid "fe514759-fa62-5f81-9d49-4904ecb413e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fece1e-fbf6-5fd9-9e1a-5416ec62c323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276536} :answer-field/id #uuid "52eacd48-b72d-58aa-9a61-50f4f444fd02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7b0ec58-a026-543f-8622-101c6f9cdc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-160419" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5754d888-a19f-5662-85cd-97ec227085bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276256} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2a7f7a-ec83-5bd9-b208-4cfc18ee1a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277128} {:answer/id #uuid "c32b2070-b78b-5733-ba17-565798a45b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277212} {:answer/id #uuid "d39eeda1-d2e0-53db-b284-9cca13530481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277344} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f8f6ed-bbe6-59b5-b0f0-7bcd01850f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5754d888-a19f-5662-85cd-97ec227085bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276256} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edd4a5b-896c-56d5-b92c-2582116c958d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09328241-3b39-5707-a62b-8ca38e3a3a9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160422" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc7595d-91e1-54d5-9f5e-676c3aaec636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275575} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5db4b5-13d0-54c7-aef2-1e1fea419adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275901} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1ea88f-ef08-5d78-bce7-bef19c3eb3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276329} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0dd3d0-fdb3-5346-b556-ee65940e8834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276594} {:answer/id #uuid "ebcb0a8d-fa30-588e-ba1c-47c666bcf358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f5db4b5-13d0-54c7-aef2-1e1fea419adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275901} :answer-field/id #uuid "57741408-8e95-5fc5-96f0-49764c243105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bb7ec9e-9e63-5c70-a122-192889149887" :question/math-academy-id "q-160444" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y(t)=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y(t)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f9743d-0bae-54e2-ac3f-9a8ef0ec7c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276565} {:answer/id #uuid "7da50905-a243-5398-a4ae-0453d1b93806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276622} {:answer/id #uuid "93e136cf-9d3b-5f55-8a6c-8a359eec69bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276817} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f674f1-c1e7-55df-9493-3907af552827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277466} {:answer/id #uuid "f54f13cc-6d1a-55a9-951b-1e16008b0a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da50905-a243-5398-a4ae-0453d1b93806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6faf48e5-84a2-5798-ac83-b8aebb344e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b24e08c4-4299-5d2f-a3af-dcce0689b7c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-160447" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x/2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x/2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & =2({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5839b9b4-19d4-5b18-aa1e-51a34ceb3a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276268} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc1e03a-b7b1-5552-a83a-8ea6f725ba18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276603} {:answer/id #uuid "87bbf739-19e8-5b24-94ae-cdbc161472cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276703} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20deef-0bff-545f-9d9a-18d430a2a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276888} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ae06da-6e7a-526b-81cc-04db2c9ea087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d20deef-0bff-545f-9d9a-18d430a2a6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276888} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bfa705-14a0-5ebd-95e5-81dc96aef48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0b61c31-2e3e-5b9a-9684-a3b763cdba2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160450" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03928662-6606-5129-a72d-197d49633a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275512} {:answer/id #uuid "36b3cff8-5ecc-5468-a7fa-6f23440457ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275964} {:answer/id #uuid "6887ebca-c248-5881-954d-54e181b800dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276413} {:answer/id #uuid "8f343f00-0285-57ae-8578-7519e66e5776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276775} {:answer/id #uuid "956e5fa9-6a49-5a82-89c8-6447bfbf9640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f343f00-0285-57ae-8578-7519e66e5776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276775} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc04777-b31e-5fbe-bdc5-27b020016731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9f1f757-0f29-5257-92da-0c605e690da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160455" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{1-2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34724edc-a4ca-5e89-a69c-bacffe903e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275945} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8e282f-89cb-53a8-9e64-f63d069a13a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276020} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2ddccb-4b30-5b76-ac15-d6c563dfdc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276287} {:answer/id #uuid "6e37991d-b419-53b8-b775-152586dd4890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276464} {:answer/id #uuid "a34f0ce9-1a9c-5f93-aaa6-3c868738beef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8e282f-89cb-53a8-9e64-f63d069a13a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276020} :answer-field/id #uuid "4646bb7e-6a09-5531-8391-f1f097bfe6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e574537-84e0-52ce-b8da-3afe1320a385" :question/math-academy-id "q-160459" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0635a288-3424-5623-8e57-1b3b518f0693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275533} {:answer/id #uuid "272eee21-fff3-5adc-b9d2-90a549a7bf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275835} {:answer/id #uuid "61d29390-173c-5987-b3dd-5c50a916cff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276360} {:answer/id #uuid "697f8c55-e019-50f8-ba40-ef76fef54d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276425} {:answer/id #uuid "6b906749-6c4e-5150-bdfb-38a2a704df05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "272eee21-fff3-5adc-b9d2-90a549a7bf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275835} :answer-field/id #uuid "46321fd4-fcba-5032-bf57-4b6cf9004b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61d3fab2-038b-5d24-9a0c-708feb5787d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160617" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,2),(1,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,-1),(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36022495-72a2-5e93-bf1a-8b5f2f33cc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275959} {:answer/id #uuid "863deeca-aa1d-5bce-9cce-0a5af1b302b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276681} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06f1dc-af3e-528c-aff2-5338c4fa84e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276752} {:answer/id #uuid "b78753dc-9d91-59ea-bfa3-225c0eb2f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277107} {:answer/id #uuid "e4950627-9582-50ba-a626-0fc32658696d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4950627-9582-50ba-a626-0fc32658696d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277474} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7fbf2c-73b0-5fd3-90e5-9b2385027897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db002c05-0ad9-5e9a-a3e6-2b51920ba6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160618" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-3),(1,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(-2,3)$\n- $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,1),(-2,3),(-1,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075cc8fc-050c-5ce2-9474-038725717320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275547} {:answer/id #uuid "099a3512-ff25-50ea-a53a-64d03f0c801f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275559} {:answer/id #uuid "4867885c-5bc5-5b3c-92ce-4ba812211ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276119} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0bec23-e660-58cb-9e9e-bf867e808af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276897} {:answer/id #uuid "c3746cdc-6312-57c7-8428-6f443a718163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3746cdc-6312-57c7-8428-6f443a718163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277215} :answer-field/id #uuid "2155e4dc-891d-5ccc-b422-ce54d6030371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11e8f9e6-6315-5288-8908-c9cd51236438" :question/math-academy-id "q-160643" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e809067-fccf-511c-9fa9-c259ab9e7737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275894} {:answer/id #uuid "40ecaffa-3314-5656-b789-5511c190e435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only"