by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-2),(1,-3),(-1,4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f0666e-f729-5d5d-985b-9b3b5b14541c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275682} {:answer/id #uuid "630a936b-d542-585d-b79f-690989f4f5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276374} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff9584-6905-5910-ae2e-3dc01139b3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277210} {:answer/id #uuid "d039d837-96b9-5df7-a439-a5936cc2ba06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277321} {:answer/id #uuid "edb63265-b8c1-54df-bd63-f9bfae318c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff9584-6905-5910-ae2e-3dc01139b3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21e65b9-a67a-5851-95df-a8435a9f7571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1a15ea-ca58-59ce-bcaf-a775bc97fe15" :question/math-academy-id "q-160645" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e457a68-368e-5519-ba70-e4e8377c24b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275596} {:answer/id #uuid "6c00bf17-6e38-54fd-bfcd-0a33163b9db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276447} {:answer/id #uuid "e5176e48-7ad7-5833-b3df-a314bb7db91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277476} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b30346-bad7-5377-822b-4463b42748de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277491} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6f6235-bc2c-5db5-a32f-903fec60bd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c00bf17-6e38-54fd-bfcd-0a33163b9db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276447} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcfe0732-cd93-5d75-b448-61744b58aa22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d53ec269-7021-530a-b0ec-0053da9223ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-160647" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,-5)$\n- $(0,2)$\n- $(-1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ef0160-9c08-5420-ac3e-f0c9a2630c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275543} {:answer/id #uuid "1049947d-3dc7-5dd0-a908-a940464ec261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275621} {:answer/id #uuid "27315988-2ebf-509d-a0df-f20a951101f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275836} {:answer/id #uuid "85eb82e3-3096-5b14-9e3e-c4284501032e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276679} {:answer/id #uuid "efbaf79c-9396-519c-9312-50554e46451d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efbaf79c-9396-519c-9312-50554e46451d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6ea799-ea45-5231-86a4-c7e103378e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97308e70-f67e-5dc9-9884-0142720ba3a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160648" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0505ac77-e2b4-55fc-93d3-b5a995a10068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275521} {:answer/id #uuid "47edec66-5b6e-5e74-b129-043bf025b643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276116} {:answer/id #uuid "69ecde40-105d-5ae7-b3a6-93c807eea4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276428} {:answer/id #uuid "86ab6a2f-0ffb-5554-a882-c016f39dc3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276686} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff3d228-d3cd-5f92-9c74-48d0f6503805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ecde40-105d-5ae7-b3a6-93c807eea4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276428} :answer-field/id #uuid "09df3955-21fb-588f-972a-b4988221d963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50910b7-4560-52e1-8087-133e3875c50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160861" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6990f532-3ef9-5d1c-8b43-3734ae7ca530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276426} {:answer/id #uuid "a6828725-95f2-5f06-a99d-e499fabb007f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276978} {:answer/id #uuid "bb784ce0-ac6e-5374-a105-c9cd31f4d905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277144} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd7ebb6-ce55-5bd2-932a-cbd136105f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277298} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1222c-fcd3-5f92-bebb-280f0a95197b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6990f532-3ef9-5d1c-8b43-3734ae7ca530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276426} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8a7159-70a6-5786-b1b8-db3d2dd689bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5fa4f79-ef60-5ce2-8e28-cc585f25eefe" :question/math-academy-id "q-160866" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0193168f-eca9-5e03-8c7b-9d2fc60f4359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275491} {:answer/id #uuid "06b0be54-86fa-5e10-babe-b83fc78943d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275540} {:answer/id #uuid "1912188d-a0c2-50ac-9c8f-b70691890eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275709} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f51112-fb20-59c8-83f8-408beaa7e96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276983} {:answer/id #uuid "e4511c58-b407-5ddd-9248-318b5042127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4511c58-b407-5ddd-9248-318b5042127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277471} :answer-field/id #uuid "98661565-ded7-54fb-9e11-58391f55aced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94165333-3657-56e4-b088-f39008c588be" :question/math-academy-id "q-160872" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- ${I}_{2}\\in R$\n- For every $A,B\\in R,$ we have $A+B\\in R.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R$ since \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwith $a=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There are matrices in $R$ whose sum is *not* in $R.$ For example, let $A=B={I}_{2}.$ Then $A,B\\in R,$  however,\n\n$$\nA+B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nsince the $11$-entry of this resulting matrix is equal to  $2\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138facaf-13f8-58b3-a787-e010ee7994bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275658} {:answer/id #uuid "26e3c102-b42e-5575-849e-e013a467a072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275830} {:answer/id #uuid "7b702978-6d75-5272-9e37-1cb1add323ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276597} {:answer/id #uuid "bf210e45-18bd-5457-a6a8-bd62a324ca33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277180} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c11f7f-a483-539c-8164-2a6ca8bf47ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9c11f7f-a483-539c-8164-2a6ca8bf47ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277270} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f8b4f5-7ce6-50f5-afcd-f9171fbcd463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e68dc301-469b-5da0-9b57-a516f231e45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160874" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $g(x)\\in C,$ where $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caad64b-1b95-5977-b4c7-4d7681b65e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275739} {:answer/id #uuid "3490413e-40bf-5073-8b46-1188bdea714b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275946} {:answer/id #uuid "70353bb0-45b7-5185-bc7f-65dc619b6d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276487} {:answer/id #uuid "b97c0539-3bd6-5b1e-a23a-a6c8bb886a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277123} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdedf5f-0e31-5305-a69c-5aff9945fd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70353bb0-45b7-5185-bc7f-65dc619b6d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276487} :answer-field/id #uuid "1feb58e1-9dc4-5428-b6c9-559c063451e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2294020-eb10-5fa0-b942-17d14bfa1ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160962" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=2$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40181228-d1a4-5f6a-ae31-3d18e7b02423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276035} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0ca9d-0d4a-5988-b9af-cee83ee8965f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276390} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9e5fe0-5935-52d6-b88c-b47ff9e397ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276891} {:answer/id #uuid "a510b60d-9c71-5128-b4f1-b4d0e5b901e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276966} {:answer/id #uuid "e28c29f0-60b2-5889-8d91-84e597eb513b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40181228-d1a4-5f6a-ae31-3d18e7b02423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276035} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a33e723-4167-587e-b7ca-38e0c3b47429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "507c7b5b-1f55-5451-94d5-1770ddfeea4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161031" :question/problem "![](images/09/0963fc3c56b5baac68e2d587896bf4655950640ecce5b924edc2d8e26a3e64e2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(u,\\,0),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/92/92bd315a07a5d76e77c249c12ea4544ee1555b0c214c68cf1f92fc3f07a71ddf.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $0$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point \n$(0,\\,0),$\nwhich is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies on the circle with radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430e8d34-03b9-591d-9c8b-3e31f116c812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276072} {:answer/id #uuid "486f4a48-a6ae-542e-b3a0-6d01f2273f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276120} {:answer/id #uuid "b61b5d70-d1e6-5e4a-82bd-2355dd672e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9abb118-15c8-5f39-ac81-9db0da50fa75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277417} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db7279b-07f0-58ff-81d0-471d2a4854aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b7f9af3-cd62-5b44-9828-72b23454ff87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161032" :question/problem "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e96dcb-b37b-59f3-a5d9-cf6b169e032f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276232} {:answer/id #uuid "587aa0bc-7ea2-57e4-8ff4-3cc53fb9b78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276270} {:answer/id #uuid "58ea32ea-2728-58c6-8af0-73ed46e3e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276277} {:answer/id #uuid "625a38d4-f891-54f4-af8a-15ab4d1ab5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276365} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fef837-46d6-5e93-a262-d3a679ae1e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "587aa0bc-7ea2-57e4-8ff4-3cc53fb9b78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276270} :answer-field/id #uuid "991a93a1-cd71-529f-b98e-a418fb4cd4fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35901d5d-b737-59bd-bd22-20c686ac3fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161035" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8afe0884ccf1e62ffdfef6cab4626bc59b3138aa5cd26f855980c88971753447.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f986685-2b9c-5ad8-afd4-5c1b7e29303d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275767} {:answer/id #uuid "332a3130-e43a-557e-8e1b-e97db157e079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275934} {:answer/id #uuid "8718f4c3-3e01-525e-9503-03e608444155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276692} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b7dec-e205-571c-a49b-c55c5660c047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276753} {:answer/id #uuid "efb3b210-9a39-5bd8-aa26-b746f808602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f986685-2b9c-5ad8-afd4-5c1b7e29303d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e96430-d1f7-5606-bae1-fae2771fbd41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b05250a-2126-54a5-8110-d92f7f2aa122" :question/math-academy-id "q-161117" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a25012c-a27b-5018-a243-b72ad59f9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275722} {:answer/id #uuid "56fe8ae8-ef09-5baf-b6d9-cf3a045b9e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276254} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec1613e-aa94-5f61-ac6a-16e3c6b423e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276628} {:answer/id #uuid "939aacfd-4fe9-5c6e-a527-af6a74544fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276814} {:answer/id #uuid "f93026a8-8e6c-5ee7-b0fb-3f8e3fa2067e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93026a8-8e6c-5ee7-b0fb-3f8e3fa2067e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277658} :answer-field/id #uuid "39edfbf2-3c27-5738-a40c-4f4be0593c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99aeb9ca-3424-5f18-af86-89763375eaeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161125" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da902a0-4f1a-5bf2-980a-2d5d456fe548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275748} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc11bb6-7c32-536f-b9d4-a691476139d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276000} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce46004-0b7a-54ce-bb9d-4fc51a7acf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276455} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdb8552-ed29-53d7-a919-cba3f9bb57fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277299} {:answer/id #uuid "e56f23fd-90a5-59c3-95dd-7f6131c93ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc11bb6-7c32-536f-b9d4-a691476139d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276000} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2f5bad-81ce-5c43-8128-293abcba8dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d909db1e-2d8a-5169-914f-b321d4c2af66" :question/math-academy-id "q-161137" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$  Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1$  and  ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f70f8e2-14a0-541d-8f3a-51fdf73fab40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276028} {:answer/id #uuid "48955f85-10da-520e-b64a-a2e6228a5686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276122} {:answer/id #uuid "79a0f10d-95cf-562e-b97e-aff8e8915577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276582} {:answer/id #uuid "97975de0-af99-571c-86b7-94a134cbf9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276841} {:answer/id #uuid "d7554c4b-94a3-5220-a3ba-b88ad1c24c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7554c4b-94a3-5220-a3ba-b88ad1c24c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277372} :answer-field/id #uuid "f653028e-d074-59c5-9d2d-bc77658240fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91da0da4-8cfa-5b1f-811f-ea50a1876fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161143" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le x\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2y+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$\n- $a=1$ and $b=2$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111589ab-eea2-5876-96d0-df31f13c4f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275628} {:answer/id #uuid "1300e133-ea83-53a7-8097-0b53d8aab763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275650} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2c1311-4b7f-55eb-8ed1-d18e3b2f7456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276169} {:answer/id #uuid "67c2eca2-ceb5-5d95-aecb-c0813f7a91ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276406} {:answer/id #uuid "fda8e7e6-61f4-58b9-a2f8-79592336f4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e2c1311-4b7f-55eb-8ed1-d18e3b2f7456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276169} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa1cc0c-c7d1-5d82-b3f9-9299432fe44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e71e8fdb-04f6-527e-89b1-2171dc56e3a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161160" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b2e528-03ef-5c07-ade8-0653d4f8351d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275807} {:answer/id #uuid "4dba8cc0-2b5d-573f-892e-a0a9501e7ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276164} {:answer/id #uuid "5431ba26-0012-55c4-9044-7aa7437596ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276223} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d3c11b-8849-5bf7-b33b-054d224f1b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276961} {:answer/id #uuid "fef33d18-b002-57ac-91d9-a222a3c54273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5431ba26-0012-55c4-9044-7aa7437596ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276223} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9a0844-6e98-5d67-aa54-3bf9816d08e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae22adfc-61f1-5211-b117-6cf267aa0468" :question/math-academy-id "q-161190" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(2x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137d263c-c389-5dd6-a081-84207ec7b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275657} {:answer/id #uuid "848e08d7-9990-576d-a7eb-6adbff3bc15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276665} {:answer/id #uuid "93da8fb6-9868-58d4-b91e-ca3e21db72ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276816} {:answer/id #uuid "9fce9e06-0f90-5722-85a9-22f3c3d9a43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276917} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9136d8-0aa9-5487-8c4f-e5cfcc2ba667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fce9e06-0f90-5722-85a9-22f3c3d9a43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276917} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3b8fe9-20b2-55f3-9ea0-bd861ecf2f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d04b955c-6599-5f09-8a8d-748ca0ca3061" :question/math-academy-id "q-161225" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c2cceb-37e1-51db-b6b0-91a43cdcf718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275707} {:answer/id #uuid "33ba9ec2-2b00-5c11-ac48-0737f7b75822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275938} {:answer/id #uuid "373b5d39-fb8a-590a-a0c7-15ed26f129a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275968} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5021bc-5ee6-5e67-ab35-0e276af2a5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277009} {:answer/id #uuid "ce936544-cae0-5e2c-8db6-7ce17099fea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce936544-cae0-5e2c-8db6-7ce17099fea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277307} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ca30a3-ee50-58f4-8a94-96d630bb562f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2955e9f2-2674-539d-b301-d0ca76c12950" :question/math-academy-id "q-161227" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095c811e-7f7e-55f1-9a53-3a726b6a583d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275557} {:answer/id #uuid "16dedc69-1d68-531a-b752-ea24759199f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275689} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7982-f074-5588-a29b-ac678935f61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275773} {:answer/id #uuid "4682dce0-636b-5a89-b9af-41c9b4bfd09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276101} {:answer/id #uuid "c8224cb2-c877-5bbf-9356-20367915e882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095c811e-7f7e-55f1-9a53-3a726b6a583d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275557} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdf8ee1-1c5b-54d1-ab38-980e9777e5f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83e332b2-7a45-565f-946e-09fe8f179dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161247" :question/problem "![](images/60/60f622b161dbfb4da1d18e85d3eb3ab4e02c2af418f9845e05a3bf9704a28006.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $x=0,$ and $x=2+y-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2-y-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=2+y-z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=5$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=2+y-z.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2af4029bd979d250ed15f351ef7f14d3ae688806db4ffec199a24b3591dadac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037775b6-1f4b-5fc7-ad27-3b9ed81d566b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275508} {:answer/id #uuid "57cf12bf-b36b-5b35-b186-38013f9ab479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276264} {:answer/id #uuid "77a6fad8-1bdc-5445-8029-fd6f8eab32c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276560} {:answer/id #uuid "9609356f-5284-5c5c-9629-638c0cfb6067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276826} {:answer/id #uuid "c40bff88-ef73-5e21-b5af-f52702082670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57cf12bf-b36b-5b35-b186-38013f9ab479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276264} :answer-field/id #uuid "155bc155-bfbc-5d0d-80ef-742677d10e35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d0077c3-5809-5281-a04f-def492b7900c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161248" :question/problem "![](images/40/40091bf7a66577a4a3f3b8cdf3b5da34cbe0e0198fdc5731c6d3dae8cb727418.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $y=0,$ and $y=4-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=4-z$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1046e312a3783dfc3253646f8a71ae226a173ac882a6305850bdd81aed9dd7e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5fc6c4-6a34-5c6d-9659-e6f6b741bbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275731} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb27137-b383-524b-854c-5b360412a95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276141} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8169d8-458c-5b8f-bcce-d1900c707665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276482} {:answer/id #uuid "90b58434-11b5-529f-831b-dda8ce34d98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276789} {:answer/id #uuid "abf8205d-26ef-5b96-8adb-5e49c300676a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5fc6c4-6a34-5c6d-9659-e6f6b741bbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275731} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6404de6-851e-554a-b5db-76be3d0e774e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bbc7b8c-0af0-5315-ba58-db2c9cd43206" :question/math-academy-id "q-161250" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d447e5a00aa324d83a3f513db11ff677fd2db2c7b1b33d8bc2bcfa73b4a08f3f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $y=0,$ and $y=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=z-\\frac{x}{3}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](images/88/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8653b6c4-8467-597d-be8d-ba8d3a520d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276682} {:answer/id #uuid "9dac6dbf-e687-5240-8a96-e42d47819580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276892} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf702c-d63d-5794-9819-34f3987f3ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277115} {:answer/id #uuid "c9935fad-acb8-5afc-adc8-1171a3d5ddca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277266} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fd63aa-c97a-5d76-9a54-d44d8fc1faab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf702c-d63d-5794-9819-34f3987f3ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277115} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1d0c1c-3d5e-5115-bd79-aad5247359fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59f1a985-1f99-5d8b-a66e-0643cac8da20" :question/math-academy-id "q-161361" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7799ca5801bf019fc5b884590575346a8f298f8e9c91056c13a60c4dd23742d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $x=0,$ and $x=4-y+2z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=4+y-2z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true and statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf8e141067fb6f675933f52e465114d8909d951f6ac85fb09c8dda7efbc2f38.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0084549c-73e8-5ee7-8d99-5487c629bcb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275485} {:answer/id #uuid "5a952ebd-2a95-53a8-8a79-0d28f11f779b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276291} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5fae5c-0bf3-5e3a-97c9-d1138edfb9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276910} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b70fad-ddad-5392-80a2-322ef8a1fd2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277114} {:answer/id #uuid "c00dd44d-d4a0-5885-a653-981f6c0eb698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a952ebd-2a95-53a8-8a79-0d28f11f779b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276291} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac60b31-2f09-5cb3-981d-cc54024348ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62e7d4a5-ede7-555e-89aa-1ccf67e4530f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161380" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614496bc-40fc-5fce-9eee-c97882e0c6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276354} {:answer/id #uuid "820d303d-e74f-5785-8189-747f90160cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276651} {:answer/id #uuid "897ae73b-74d6-5877-9823-73d7dd30e83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276717} {:answer/id #uuid "ab20cd1c-bfe5-5479-baa6-bc2544a804f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277014} {:answer/id #uuid "dac8e224-91b8-574a-80e2-eca923719ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897ae73b-74d6-5877-9823-73d7dd30e83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276717} :answer-field/id #uuid "c789861f-2604-592d-b92e-61be5fffa117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a480e12-228f-5db7-81b6-a3f28cbc1181" :question/math-academy-id "q-161386" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$\n- $a=-3$ and $b=3$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06342551-0e88-5a6b-bd0d-60e7e94f92cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275532} {:answer/id #uuid "514cdd25-4769-5166-850c-a323f4ce90d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276204} {:answer/id #uuid "9472a39b-33fc-53d0-8cc5-e0e82c1a530a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276820} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d437f7-5a15-5e16-8d18-d6085aacba13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276973} {:answer/id #uuid "e15fd5f3-6d8f-5ca6-a7db-f5eec29b87ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06342551-0e88-5a6b-bd0d-60e7e94f92cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275532} :answer-field/id #uuid "0377995e-0aa9-5059-b2c1-6fd8c5abf106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2307ae9e-a249-5090-bfed-df0660f7b0eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161601" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1762dd7a-cdff-5568-977d-6d80f2adc2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275698} {:answer/id #uuid "89802e03-4038-5079-a2d6-6fbcbfaa4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276719} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30765e-36b9-5ec6-98f6-842bac2a8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276741} {:answer/id #uuid "a64a6892-a119-5289-a94b-626583cc70ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276975} {:answer/id #uuid "af9e721c-402a-5c3f-9692-3951b59def5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89802e03-4038-5079-a2d6-6fbcbfaa4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276719} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45260eb-ab24-5e89-9cc0-6905bd2dcc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4104ec23-658c-58b7-8dbf-72086dab259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161622" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21691c25-85fe-50d4-8992-94793dce0cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275780} {:answer/id #uuid "25bc0f7d-35fe-5f7e-8ee1-1db7cf2b62c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b4bc38-7d7f-5677-a106-02e57bec4368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276211} {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc124b6-2d46-5e4f-a491-7d52913d1bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ee44d9-0fea-51a9-b385-74b8a13e1ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161624" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51545767-3cc2-5437-9f48-638348ea4cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276205} {:answer/id #uuid "65e476b0-8d46-5284-adff-7ab3dfc01d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276391} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5f5c40-cedb-5337-a6d5-fdb5d40c8667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276728} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbc21fd-75c6-57b1-b5df-f0a1cd6cf1c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277540} {:answer/id #uuid "fbea97be-23e3-55aa-9854-424f79b2abcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65e476b0-8d46-5284-adff-7ab3dfc01d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276391} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c6778a-20db-511f-af8b-42a6afc7a710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be0484aa-ac87-5330-9ede-cd54e1386a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-161625" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2-x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ee6128-4b12-5d48-8dff-b37815d7c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276067} {:answer/id #uuid "616176be-43c2-5bf1-9551-04f24aa9c7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276357} {:answer/id #uuid "93754e25-73f5-5f71-a810-562bad5c14e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276812} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d17a5a-3c3f-5175-8d28-45025dc8c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199} {:answer/id #uuid "f8693685-82a4-5f06-bb61-c83ee0c7838f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d17a5a-3c3f-5175-8d28-45025dc8c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7cf686-3dda-5407-8450-9e265af612dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "baee2889-195d-5ff5-b660-0a9acac1604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161626" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/45/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](images/2a/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ec5642-511f-525b-ac0a-770318939699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275681} {:answer/id #uuid "7623462b-d6fb-53e4-a6d0-244ab142865f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276543} {:answer/id #uuid "81e99452-886a-5c7a-8df1-ffa01b9aedd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276649} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ea606-4938-581a-b235-1d5a03d8ffb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b651c8-5a34-50dd-b948-339f9951003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b651c8-5a34-50dd-b948-339f9951003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277642} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fcbcb7-ae99-53f0-9014-81699073744c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fb05970-59dc-5904-9b90-0c1d735b4269" :question/math-academy-id "q-161638" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<2-{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88fe12a7-69f1-5681-a35b-e338f545c46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276713} {:answer/id #uuid "c2180e8f-4b9a-5aa2-9740-6862795df816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277202} {:answer/id #uuid "ce490ba2-fe22-5d7a-91b3-06c3ed53dbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277303} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aa9c7c-2f3a-5bb8-ad00-d6a3b212e70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277605} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cb1adb-4be0-5edc-b104-f304ae26284e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3cb1adb-4be0-5edc-b104-f304ae26284e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277613} :answer-field/id #uuid "0499070a-047d-5992-bdca-50f8c7b2a165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20984d3c-4813-50f9-a16b-ba04fcaf3833" :question/math-academy-id "q-161640" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-3<x<-1,\\,1<y<0.5x+3}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6099c711-d3a9-564f-bb4a-99eabc4151f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276342} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7b4754-6ba3-5316-9b68-6c6a99b8415e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276882} {:answer/id #uuid "ce49e8b7-51b1-5fd9-b7f4-352cc69fb5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277304} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1e51db-a986-5045-8a5c-72ccbc30643d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277556} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7f93ff-89eb-54b6-9af8-86fe18fa0ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1e51db-a986-5045-8a5c-72ccbc30643d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277556} :answer-field/id #uuid "675386e0-318d-5cea-9a65-ffcd8c75b72e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ae695d6-ab71-5a57-95a1-94d938d5de7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161641" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<x<2,\\,0<y<\\frac{1}{x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<\\sqrt{1-x}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 8,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018bc100-91aa-543a-936f-53e746dca2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275489} {:answer/id #uuid "195e644d-a593-5f9b-af66-b2aa1bce32c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275714} {:answer/id #uuid "26adde98-a760-53b4-92f0-8185d373a841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275828} {:answer/id #uuid "6b905fcf-9f51-5621-8ee6-8290966ae390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276442} {:answer/id #uuid "f6255c1f-fd9d-576d-b0e6-6836e1b6e132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195e644d-a593-5f9b-af66-b2aa1bce32c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275714} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a32781a-dbff-5332-93de-1878429e1d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "168b395c-d069-5c8f-9acc-5bed4b37b963" :question/math-academy-id "q-161642" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} {:answer/id #uuid "72118cf0-8b8a-5295-919c-27f5cbbdd835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276510} {:answer/id #uuid "87dc8626-1873-5666-be98-d2e39efb8926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276704} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f3455-f66a-5215-a435-be8ebbaf60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276950} {:answer/id #uuid "bc63d771-387a-52c7-99fd-876c0770a2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bbeb67-b26e-5d94-986d-584288de76a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa6cde3-1ae8-5afa-8dd7-e176e90cdc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275902} {:answer/id #uuid "36daff44-c75d-5e4b-84a4-fc89decd9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275965} {:answer/id #uuid "8317ebc3-52a8-5268-806e-9ea55699a12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276656} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e4f0f1-29dc-5a28-9987-f0bbdea2ac72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276991} {:answer/id #uuid "abb406d7-4768-5e61-bbbb-9a6d024ea5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb406d7-4768-5e61-bbbb-9a6d024ea5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277020} :answer-field/id #uuid "4419feba-604a-59e5-b277-df3d6b966710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3f47e10-181c-587e-a0be-2b1e263782c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161646" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\,0<y<|2x|}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f43270e-5fd5-5fe3-b96d-2845968d906e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275762} {:answer/id #uuid "91704fdf-0254-5001-b4f6-f9904429324d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276793} {:answer/id #uuid "ade20714-3320-54e9-8da1-4e2411c22400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277039} {:answer/id #uuid "edcf9c63-cf2d-53a3-bf2a-463a56016f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277565} {:answer/id #uuid "ede4ae0d-2c59-565f-940e-2e848fb5dec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edcf9c63-cf2d-53a3-bf2a-463a56016f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277565} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda39969-1d4f-5486-b042-72e33847bb7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdca40a5-5172-5279-8887-174acedd2d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-161647" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are open?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a444db7-d5d6-57e6-8e48-407211cee868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275723} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1bbcf0-1dcb-5285-af5d-b33113f92715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275745} {:answer/id #uuid "3316cb25-b494-5e84-8c8c-14602033695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275932} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c15056-e18f-5bda-8f30-b2945d8e51a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277103} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0c797b-7971-52bb-a227-fbf910934ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c15056-e18f-5bda-8f30-b2945d8e51a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277103} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c97ad8-2e05-54f3-9147-887f2a79b763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91207617-bfa9-5efa-a562-b7d640b0c541" :question/math-academy-id "q-161651" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aabbcf16598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278638} {:answer/id #uuid "21f1b1b1-4cbf-5384-abf1-011fb4b8e732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278897} {:answer/id #uuid "62d28ecd-0906-545c-93e9-7da6ccef73dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279488} {:answer/id #uuid "84b65459-6cd3-563c-a465-2e40b3ffdf57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279785} {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48d194b-9d36-54e9-be97-ae912aef2d30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\n- $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24e418-85ae-5620-871f-6fadf0d095e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278697} {:answer/id #uuid "29c92160-c1eb-56ed-9791-f6e1013149b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278961} {:answer/id #uuid "8253b24f-d960-5879-a212-1945f39d7682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279759} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec6c6ee-706a-5375-90e8-ede072b04c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279862} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d68eed-e257-52f7-9b26-4c25a946c054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c92160-c1eb-56ed-9791-f6e1013149b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a894510-e33f-5f5d-8250-b299a0d3e001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9d32a6-dbbc-54ba-9b9d-46f810008dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b4672d-68a5-5528-9942-1664c8cc41c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279624} {:answer/id #uuid "9850165e-020e-5c66-9df2-3f70060031d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279936} {:answer/id #uuid "be7f6526-1ee8-5f9a-a85e-5b94893d66a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280266} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcad550-3086-5eb3-a21d-444940b41b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280566} {:answer/id #uuid "ea58b8ad-1ed8-5961-8482-503dd54e5ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b4672d-68a5-5528-9942-1664c8cc41c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279624} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d2ac1f-f189-5f14-86c2-c852d3262ba2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-161857" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "370a4942-1f76-59dc-92de-398986da154a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279072} {:answer/id #uuid "6818c055-c5bc-597d-a79f-c1f633ffb68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279538} {:answer/id #uuid "6b574d41-77de-52a6-9437-6ace3f2aedd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279567} {:answer/id #uuid "8c181626-6a30-553b-aa88-3b952ab1aaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279841} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae78b3-944c-5c2f-ae28-6d83e509df06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ae78b3-944c-5c2f-ae28-6d83e509df06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279934} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a65fa65-1952-5ab3-bc27-708d7ce3f427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "649157e5-32cb-5583-98fc-af0b21c39379" :question/math-academy-id "q-161858" :question/problem "![](images/54/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "213210b9-7728-530d-a963-d3ff65be676e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278892} {:answer/id #uuid "402bd606-65e8-5fa3-ab52-0b7cc337071f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279159} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ae2a0f-bfd4-55d4-99c4-f0ccde065b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280225} {:answer/id #uuid "d71c5cc6-7488-5caf-93c9-29f01f69ca84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280491} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f7b247-0979-57a5-bbc0-c01b77d20cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ae2a0f-bfd4-55d4-99c4-f0ccde065b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5fea5f4-2d0b-572f-ad9e-0133983bb7d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c687527-ddc3-50e7-9a3f-531649897edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161865" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0.5,1.5),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-0.88=f(0),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is also false. Notice that $y=2.5=f(3).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=3$ since ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f8763f-3161-5551-aa35-73532e492605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278633} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9886ae-f980-5a00-9c1a-057bd03547a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278999} {:answer/id #uuid "8227cae4-c91d-51e1-8980-ee0f7f28c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279758} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8f4100-30ef-5b86-9c36-81553d8eee7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280119} {:answer/id #uuid "f378f7c1-4542-5fc2-abb2-a3fde2c92d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f8763f-3161-5551-aa35-73532e492605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278633} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a1f0136-b12c-5079-a0db-5ef13aa52899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eff559e-2582-52e2-ae0e-c082d3e9afb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161866" :question/problem "![](images/67/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=1=f(-1).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1$ since ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=0=f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(1,3),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=2,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(2)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01683696-1768-510b-b2ef-229cf3c6ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278595} {:answer/id #uuid "21a3a624-267c-5525-b0d6-fa9087ace471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278895} {:answer/id #uuid "578cbb0d-4b00-595c-8c1f-db8ea0aabe49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279395} {:answer/id #uuid "678ad038-6468-51ae-9b8c-30158d54f8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279530} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bbf243-f303-5b79-9edf-c8d2849b004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "678ad038-6468-51ae-9b8c-30158d54f8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39dcd4a-7825-5c92-92a2-098ef045e0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46fbce01-7db5-5630-8364-44d4a151bbf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162346" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $4$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${w}^{4}+1=1$\n- ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$\n- $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{w}^{4}+1=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4} & =\\frac{{z}^{4}}{{w}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1,$ which means that $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the quotient of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159fcf09-f3bf-5eae-8ee5-70cac24cfdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278785} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2aa89f-0fb1-552e-bcf1-e5d22bff96c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279107} {:answer/id #uuid "48982c83-3829-5a01-a1b7-8bff71aa1b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279239} {:answer/id #uuid "89a66cea-cc4e-5d42-b1bd-86ad16a6ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279817} {:answer/id #uuid "c9733f31-89dd-59b5-bc59-6b88a5d62b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9733f31-89dd-59b5-bc59-6b88a5d62b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db6fc7b-b924-5f9c-9c1d-950c074bfbb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b77c81df-773f-534f-b1e3-2174eb2531c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162347" :question/problem "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $({z}^{3})^{2}=-1$\n- ${z}^{3}$ is a $6$th root of unity\n- ${z}^{3}$ is a square root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{2}={z}^{6}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{6} & =({z}^{6})^{3} \\\\  & =(1)^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a $6$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{3})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066100ae-d403-5565-b092-18ed907cf446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278650} {:answer/id #uuid "3a21f67b-04e9-51b6-b2f2-87910d42dba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279106} {:answer/id #uuid "8a168bae-f461-5782-acce-733c8d30db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279824} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4173bf-d317-5b23-9d1c-9ee28afe9ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279979} {:answer/id #uuid "f50fc075-cdb1-5948-b267-cdca2c9b236f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066100ae-d403-5565-b092-18ed907cf446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278650} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fde1e26-7b8a-5610-85f5-454d3ec9a8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b299b05-4c26-5412-a581-f913a340d791" :question/math-academy-id "q-162348" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $8$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{8}=1$\n- $(zw)^{8}=1$\n- $zw$ is an $8$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $z$ and $w$ are $8$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{8}={w}^{8}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{8} & ={z}^{8}{w}^{8} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(zw)^{8}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is an $8$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0562b352-4bde-5c23-b5a1-ae34aaafb142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278640} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3e4451-82d0-5454-b503-9a3725bcfabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279553} {:answer/id #uuid "a7453063-397e-5c14-a2f3-c8d6f10fdf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280073} {:answer/id #uuid "bdef9375-0239-53a2-bd45-8e2529f94cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280260} {:answer/id #uuid "d22bd3b9-17de-5f4d-baf8-343dd0530797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3e4451-82d0-5454-b503-9a3725bcfabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279553} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69f0859-b3c4-5c51-a52a-95afdd5e344f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a65d62e-3083-571b-b429-c51c7b117795" :question/math-academy-id "q-162349" :question/problem "Let $z$ be an $8$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $({z}^{4})^{2}=-1$\n- ${z}^{4}$ is an $8$th root of unity\n- ${z}^{4}$ is a square root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({z}^{4})}^{2}={z}^{8}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{4})^{8} & =({z}^{8})^{4} \\\\  & =(1)^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a $8$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{4})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16feb184-d357-53c7-9778-5e97cf1ca335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278796} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee0df5-f222-5b38-a65b-436a15c274bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279470} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcee9e6-8b9c-53ad-988c-edb5886dbf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607} {:answer/id #uuid "c412b129-79de-5309-8d14-fb3c7439cc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280317} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc4a36e-3a93-5957-9b39-9f8653de46ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fcee9e6-8b9c-53ad-988c-edb5886dbf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4c8f7f-28c0-58d5-8cf8-12c5e21d0394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec73ee18-5d78-5b94-8905-bcb7e7e3a771" :question/math-academy-id "q-162350" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $4$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{4}=-1$\n- $(zw)^{4}=0$\n- $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{4} & ={z}^{4}{w}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have seen above that $(zw)^{4}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e3d6a-4d9f-5068-9677-962c469778a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278589} {:answer/id #uuid "a71ddf06-c1d1-599f-8807-654a1bc2a38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280070} {:answer/id #uuid "c017cd9c-fa9b-584a-a3e5-167b21b4d322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280283} {:answer/id #uuid "ef044fb1-8503-582f-808e-4db0ada40bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280686} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d312db-c3d1-5a33-ac03-8991872badaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef044fb1-8503-582f-808e-4db0ada40bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280686} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b19619f-e593-5557-99d9-f07302fd0b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6154f21e-3dc4-5e29-9ac5-5bb6e4c1043c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162351" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be cube roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{3}=1$\n- $(z+z)^{3}=8$\n- $w+w=2w$ is a cube root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ and $w$ are cube roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{3}={w}^{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{3} & =(2z)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(w+w)^{3}=8\\ne 1,$ which means that $w+w=2w$ is *not* a cube root of unity. \n\nIn general, the sum of $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e097dc-5cbc-5475-a347-f736fa0a0085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279064} {:answer/id #uuid "627e72ad-78d6-5cd0-92a1-f250ec9e6398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279487} {:answer/id #uuid "77023edc-8fd5-533e-8f0f-06f6de498f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279654} {:answer/id #uuid "9675a4e0-e229-53aa-b0a5-f65cc721c6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279924} {:answer/id #uuid "deabc480-dd74-54b7-b0a6-0681f65b4aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e097dc-5cbc-5475-a347-f736fa0a0085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279064} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddb2faf-6904-5f9d-83dc-ccae19db486b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10662523-d7b1-54f8-907f-f332605eaff8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162368" :question/problem "Let $z$ be a $4$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{4}=-1$\n- $(z+z)^{4}=16$\n- $z+z=2z$  is not a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ is a  $4$th root of unity, then (by definition) we have that\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{4} & =(2z)^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {z}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^{4}\\ne 1,$ which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $4$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c51958-8ef4-52c7-b869-046cef0ac581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278618} {:answer/id #uuid "8971b69c-7d3f-5ca3-a1b5-940a8d208ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279815} {:answer/id #uuid "a17a122f-18bb-5811-9a74-7caf81094891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280027} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ae3ed5-1e5a-5b4e-be5c-c40bf41bbf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280313} {:answer/id #uuid "f629aa2d-8006-58cb-9244-a7c7bf8bf97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a17a122f-18bb-5811-9a74-7caf81094891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280027} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d02ff57-cf82-5c69-b72f-570a5d874496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b7bc6df-b3cb-50da-83fc-955ab52a05f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162369" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $6$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $z{w}^{6}=w$\n- $(zw)^{6}=1$\n- $zw$ is not a $6$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,  since $z$ and $w$ are $6$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{6}={w}^{6}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\nz{w}^{6}=z\\cdot 1=z\\ne w.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{6} & ={z}^{6}{w}^{6} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have seen above that $(zw)^{6}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $6$th root of unity.  \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fae4e2-b796-5141-b87c-f1b4312776c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278623} {:answer/id #uuid "560257e8-1ca3-572c-91a0-bf9c4c6479ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279377} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f53f36-9565-5df6-a9bf-bbb66fe548d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280032} {:answer/id #uuid "c134a8e8-ec53-5f35-9c7d-c9248aaa4b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280288} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1f42d-1d9e-5704-971e-9225dd7bfa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fae4e2-b796-5141-b87c-f1b4312776c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35c9888-1ae6-5550-8edd-912098edf6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d62a3796-3a93-54ec-8c55-9e54ca670215" :question/math-academy-id "q-162379" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in N,$ where $p(x)={x}^{3}-1.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c955f2-1f95-51a2-98f0-3eb576e26d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278744} {:answer/id #uuid "2799adbd-5b80-54b8-b771-04a4880c4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278946} {:answer/id #uuid "79086227-e4ae-5a6a-b5a3-f4679b5f6866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279670} {:answer/id #uuid "d1456bc7-eb62-5a81-b906-11f9d53e6ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280438} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e1527a-7305-51ee-83a2-83652b17baf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1456bc7-eb62-5a81-b906-11f9d53e6ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280438} :answer-field/id #uuid "eccd1f91-0329-5827-8bc7-20841f3abdcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9060a6c-635e-5bea-91ee-1f014ee29783" :question/math-academy-id "q-162391" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant different from $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The  matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have $A+(B-C)=(A+B)-C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with real entries,   there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice if $A\\in S$ then \n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot |A|=|A|\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in S,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+(B-C)=A+(B+(-C))=(A+B)+(-C)=(A+B)-C\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ad865a-c3b5-5e64-b983-c2bbdb3b6af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279375} {:answer/id #uuid "86c7827e-434f-553f-88c9-aaa62d97cbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279796} {:answer/id #uuid "b59e4cea-9d7c-5d22-9635-07ebe2760eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280189} {:answer/id #uuid "cb948721-37d5-577b-8333-945399a90c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280375} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae77ea-1bc8-57fa-af15-b2b0fd2f630b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb948721-37d5-577b-8333-945399a90c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280375} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e076d65-0526-5a07-9c9b-b679b4af936f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b2f6345-ac14-55a4-a687-5a14893fcf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-162392" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)-q(x)=q(x)-p(x).$\n- There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}+0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3}\\in U$ and the zero-polynomial $q(x)=0\\in U.$ Then \n\n$$\np(x)-q(x)=p(x)-0={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\nq(x)-p(x)=0-p(x)=-{x}^{3}\\ne {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd94e17-bd33-5b30-aa5a-328c6b789d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278703} {:answer/id #uuid "3143490e-860f-54af-8e94-5f4056f483e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279024} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb893ab-6e11-554e-a7be-fa20c957f926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279579} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6153df-8074-581c-b9da-1f1a4795fedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279958} {:answer/id #uuid "c42042f3-b109-5b62-a7e7-b8de00e7a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a6153df-8074-581c-b9da-1f1a4795fedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279958} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd00186b-59bb-5767-9390-25942cf1323a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b6ba3e9-8414-5dea-8a72-007f56c21b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162530" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/d6/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d18e52-30b5-50e8-9ff5-8e526ca19e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278804} {:answer/id #uuid "1c301707-4934-595d-8f02-af50fd610d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278850} {:answer/id #uuid "1c651566-b11a-5bb8-a043-0a9fafb2b206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278853} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6009ac-5a10-58f6-aeca-b06c31d51116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280658} {:answer/id #uuid "f83b45be-ad2c-52fd-a929-fe2a0b7ff0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6009ac-5a10-58f6-aeca-b06c31d51116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280658} :answer-field/id #uuid "430f4680-3a42-5091-a55a-d38a44cf8c20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ab2ad7f-5552-5a07-8645-8d079346b7d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162620" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero matrix $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $G.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in G,$ we have $A-O=A.$\n- For any $A,B\\in G,$ we have $A-B=B-A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2048c5b4-3996-5c47-9a17-90d26d347fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278889} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef2145c-b1f4-56c4-9959-10d10c087ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279000} {:answer/id #uuid "762200d2-8ac3-54b0-a844-d5ae31f4789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279645} {:answer/id #uuid "dddcd65b-9ce6-50ce-9228-6bcf5cea7db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} {:answer/id #uuid "e26ca2e7-67dd-5dc7-a4ef-404b70019c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dddcd65b-9ce6-50ce-9228-6bcf5cea7db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280548} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eae0065-06b1-55e5-93fa-33a43fe9d0d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff69a5c1-505a-5719-b6b9-7ec69e0931ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162664" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b086d6-282d-539c-a57d-391d0dec58ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278630} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa822f7-a755-5bc2-a4c0-162ea80a54d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278694} {:answer/id #uuid "62f1b9cd-f6c3-567d-83a1-8747b28c88e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279490} {:answer/id #uuid "889f55cf-6e23-52fa-b140-228200799b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279812} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e12186-da0b-532c-aea5-5d654db558d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04b086d6-282d-539c-a57d-391d0dec58ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278630} :answer-field/id #uuid "189145d5-9042-5aff-b66a-167759c966a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69e25145-91f1-58d1-afd8-be54a0c5863e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162665" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42fb6015-4e55-5e59-89d9-b10ee43c6b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279193} {:answer/id #uuid "630f962f-ad64-5905-b1c9-55f2c1ee2d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279492} {:answer/id #uuid "7c41e5e0-acec-56d4-bec0-acafe42eb59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279700} {:answer/id #uuid "add1f3d3-85fe-5fe3-8141-536af462dcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280131} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1ca911-5fad-599e-a7b6-64e46293a25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1ca911-5fad-599e-a7b6-64e46293a25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280687} :answer-field/id #uuid "671306cf-68c7-52e8-a31a-0158cf1c261e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2124447c-ee22-58ee-9afe-87a9532907c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162668" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $A\\in Y,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ we have $2A\\in Y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {a}_{ij}=-{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=-A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Y,$ however $2{I}_{2}\\notin Y$ because\n\n$$\n|2{I}_{2}|=|2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0=4\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12876655-bd13-566f-a850-1b77fec4f132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278762} {:answer/id #uuid "31065572-96df-5949-b308-0ff40294f839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279020} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee88460-99ed-5931-a1e1-2386fcc54e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279309} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b10179-d4e1-543d-9c16-e97b713cd841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280707} {:answer/id #uuid "f92cfc6e-a430-5c29-9e0e-a4bbfd8e6d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12876655-bd13-566f-a850-1b77fec4f132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278762} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9c8ac01-ec8d-5027-bb4a-610633cf0ccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5dd282b-2dab-5297-89c3-a4849921b265" :question/math-academy-id "q-162746" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.06$ | $0.04$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.06.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.3=0.06.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.1\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105f9328-6fea-56d5-904d-57060e998bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278740} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1d9431-e04e-564b-aa8a-435658fea4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279105} {:answer/id #uuid "5b96e947-0465-51e5-9134-3329a964ca67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279434} {:answer/id #uuid "c15362f9-556d-5395-8e64-173070c68e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280291} {:answer/id #uuid "cd312962-d86a-57ef-9dda-dc306846c347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b96e947-0465-51e5-9134-3329a964ca67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279434} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd327cdd-d129-557c-811c-e39819071b8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "745e2846-c808-51fa-a431-23f636897e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-162824" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=0)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=0)=f(1,0)=0.25.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=0)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf75ab2-772c-57da-b308-392f0140c5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278707} {:answer/id #uuid "236e1c39-9749-57ed-ad04-dfcf440292ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278909} {:answer/id #uuid "5f819bdf-1c20-593f-83ef-4d1c6dd3cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279468} {:answer/id #uuid "867cb1fc-c66f-5ce5-bf7f-8b64eb519bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279792} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4d654-5df4-51ef-9432-ac240b22f91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f819bdf-1c20-593f-83ef-4d1c6dd3cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279468} :answer-field/id #uuid "e308b1a0-ff07-540c-b063-07bba0eb3e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b24b5d8-591a-5107-953f-3194f9f2c074" :question/math-academy-id "q-163605" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{24}$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | |  | | $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=-2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=-2)=f(0,-2)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =\\frac{5}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,-2) & =\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,2) & =\\frac{5}{24}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f81ad3a-844d-53cf-a650-4b87d84a63bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282642} {:answer/id #uuid "e6078d6a-5cb8-5491-a612-8430e2a0076f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283694} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b17e8-dc3d-53ea-be09-31383ec00628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283777} {:answer/id #uuid "efc14eb8-f223-526f-b439-350d536f1b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283778} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c0adb-ce8e-54d8-9ded-53bbd3569ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6078d6a-5cb8-5491-a612-8430e2a0076f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283694} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21b0450-9e09-55ba-b93a-884d35439646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25bf9bc6-630d-50c0-9382-20c9fae3e81f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163617" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $0.22$ | $0.33$ |\n| $X=8$ | $0.18$ | $0.27$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ | |  | | $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=8,Y=1)=P(X=8)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=8,Y=1)=f(8,1)=0.27.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=8)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.45\\cdot 0.6=0.27.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =0.22=0.55\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,1) & =0.33=0.55\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(8,-1) & =0.18=0.45\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18917fd5-fee6-57f3-9a0d-e99e67957d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281911} {:answer/id #uuid "45d8ecc2-5b67-5aa1-b375-522a764588b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282278} {:answer/id #uuid "8050de08-95e2-5f0e-9fb2-7a9048afbf9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282794} {:answer/id #uuid "a3823492-cef8-5116-8744-6833125e4fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283113} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2749f4-88f1-52ec-86e4-6ac7a71c3e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3823492-cef8-5116-8744-6833125e4fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283113} :answer-field/id #uuid "8477fe82-1845-53eb-b27a-addfd1449854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59d86f24-1bbb-55d7-8154-a7330272c0b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163779" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0f9401-75e4-55a8-b07b-42f0a4d45d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281791} {:answer/id #uuid "307fe352-fb4f-5bb8-98cd-4af89d96ac4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282119} {:answer/id #uuid "4db5deee-ad0b-5ef1-8767-984c9a923c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282359} {:answer/id #uuid "577764d6-f929-53e9-835e-ee0587b2f33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282434} {:answer/id #uuid "59e6ef8e-5539-5c18-adac-4bdf812c7e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577764d6-f929-53e9-835e-ee0587b2f33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282434} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e2152f-856c-5c1a-8f4e-2490e8651642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03651691-7356-5261-96f0-0d0badb00da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163780" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11da9ba8-b0fc-5575-8516-ebc73d7110f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281859} {:answer/id #uuid "18052fff-a476-55c1-9a9b-a566937c4c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281906} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5f5869-08c9-5d4d-8d0f-dc7f8e2b4a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282108} {:answer/id #uuid "3acc1f61-b885-5711-ab62-91ffba20c5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282208} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5ef0fb-4857-5d66-b388-8e8383ac0236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18052fff-a476-55c1-9a9b-a566937c4c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281906} :answer-field/id #uuid "afac7bf0-5be1-5440-9c97-cdab5c8a167b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c598e2d2-d26a-59ea-a0e5-a7e53e350ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ce76af-205c-5f15-9bf3-8a07b4ec1e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281722} {:answer/id #uuid "1b24e6dc-b5d1-59ba-b70f-33fbb80b50ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281935} {:answer/id #uuid "55e1a80d-87b4-5087-aee8-8b2844123c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282418} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2193f7-4427-54bb-ad82-a4f889b9fc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283304} {:answer/id #uuid "cab1e93b-05b8-5fa6-8e26-02d8417d7a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b24e6dc-b5d1-59ba-b70f-33fbb80b50ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281935} :answer-field/id #uuid "351b3660-3340-57a7-a9d4-9d1c13ce632a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00b2158a-941e-5a2f-b68c-c230bee6df19" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=-19$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317831e4-d61d-5565-8a1d-8ebe215ce723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282133} {:answer/id #uuid "6646085a-3141-5ad3-8ba9-d6918cfd6d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282571} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa73da7-0cb2-5e05-9a53-9ec5af708ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282609} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae9e39c-25b7-5cc1-930c-d362143f9226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282887} {:answer/id #uuid "f439a8b5-3665-522b-ba36-77757135d2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aa73da7-0cb2-5e05-9a53-9ec5af708ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282609} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6317201-7dc4-5d57-ab22-3d51d5c87106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce7de24-db57-5ead-8c61-93889fcd8ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163812" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab9428d-b800-5bca-860f-b8fd5a8017dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281933} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8f5b01-a4ed-5d81-825b-4359a907b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282220} {:answer/id #uuid "96ac3337-5fcf-585d-aa53-df945f0c1697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282995} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f82781-85bb-5bac-a3c2-f04a7e43abdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283436} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc690d7-23a4-5ff0-960d-729131523e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c8f5b01-a4ed-5d81-825b-4359a907b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282220} :answer-field/id #uuid "c503a37b-08be-5f34-abac-499195c355a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8eeb45f2-bc46-5681-a60d-c808242e288d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164138" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34aec981-91ea-59e4-a4ad-1204aef5e40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282159} {:answer/id #uuid "4314674b-64e2-5c3d-a8a3-59d7a831302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282260} {:answer/id #uuid "580cea9a-913a-5c10-abc2-0aa039c35c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282439} {:answer/id #uuid "8f093edf-21a9-5516-ace3-b68c06905f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282929} {:answer/id #uuid "966c8f21-0659-5e10-ab74-318e37c8ef6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "966c8f21-0659-5e10-ab74-318e37c8ef6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282991} :answer-field/id #uuid "694264e5-9bbc-593e-8e54-187a6aec189b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "140468c2-8315-56a7-adfc-2601c4482a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-164150" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdfae6a5a321d1dbc235d06438847d5a0cc471fa4e31575f36c3967dca239824.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/82/82291f89bd32f3a3a20d39dfc89de91b2f673bec7c802cebf6d5fa3b4136ace1.png)"}]
